WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.249
Software supports everyday activities, and a
large system can exceed what any one developer

00:00:05.249 --> 00:00:10.556
can understand at once. The historical codebase
examples in the slides illustrate growth and

00:00:10.556 --> 00:00:15.863
complexity. Their exact line counts are
snapshots, not a measure of design quality or a
rule

00:00:15.863 --> 00:00:18.171
for when architecture becomes necessary.

00:00:18.171 --> 00:00:23.372
Complexity creates practical questions: where
should a change go, what might it break, how

00:00:23.372 --> 00:00:28.516
can we inspect security and quality, and how
will the system run and evolve? Architecture

00:00:28.516 --> 00:00:33.602
provides a manageable account of important
parts, their relationships, and the decisions

00:00:33.602 --> 00:00:38.342
that constrain them. It connects a problem and
its requirements to a solution that

00:00:38.342 --> 00:00:40.307
developers can implement and test.

00:00:40.307 --> 00:00:45.488
The engineering analogy in the slides emphasizes
planning, coordination, and checking the

00:00:45.488 --> 00:00:50.552
result. It does not require every decision to be
completed before coding. Architectural

00:00:50.552 --> 00:00:55.733
reasoning can continue as implementation and
feedback reveal new constraints. Maintenance

00:00:55.733 --> 00:01:00.972
and evolution make preserving the reasons behind
important decisions valuable. Two systems

00:01:00.972 --> 00:01:03.126
have the same number of source lines.

00:01:03.307 --> 00:01:08.812
Must they need the same architectural effort?
No. Relationships, stakeholder concerns,

00:01:08.812 --> 00:01:14.637
quality requirements, and the cost of changing
important decisions matter. Line count alone

00:01:14.637 --> 00:01:16.558
does not reveal these factors.

00:01:16.807 --> 00:01:21.868
A system exists in an environment and serves a
mission. Its stakeholders are people or

00:01:21.868 --> 00:01:27.047
organizations with an interest in its behavior
or development. For a museum catalog, the

00:01:27.047 --> 00:01:32.227
mission might be to help visitors discover
exhibits; curators, visitors, and maintainers

00:01:32.227 --> 00:01:33.639
have different concerns.

00:01:33.807 --> 00:01:39.008
State the system boundary so that external
people and systems are not mistaken for internal

00:01:39.008 --> 00:01:39.637
components.

00:01:39.807 --> 00:01:45.121
The definitions in the slides emphasize
components, relationships, interfaces, behavior,

00:01:45.121 --> 00:01:50.254
composition, and guiding principles.
Architectural decisions are the design decisions

00:01:50.254 --> 00:01:55.809
significant to the system as a whole: they shape
how major responsibilities fit together and

00:01:55.809 --> 00:02:01.244
how important qualities can be achieved.
Architecture is design, but a local design
choice

00:02:01.244 --> 00:02:04.203
is not automatically architecturally
significant.

00:02:04.307 --> 00:02:10.013
Significance depends on context. An algorithm or
data structure can become an architectural

00:02:10.013 --> 00:02:15.407
concern if it determines system-wide
performance, compatibility, or feasible
behavior.

00:02:15.407 --> 00:02:20.674
Conversely, a small local implementation choice
may be easy to replace. Architecture

00:02:20.674 --> 00:02:26.068
supports communication among stakeholders,
analysis of a proposed system, and reuse of

00:02:26.068 --> 00:02:31.147
successful structures; a diagram is useful only
when people agree on its meaning.

00:02:31.307 --> 00:02:36.547
Is choosing an algorithm always outside software
architecture? No. A locally replaceable

00:02:36.547 --> 00:02:41.907
algorithm may be detailed design, but one that
constrains the whole system or determines a

00:02:41.907 --> 00:02:45.003
critical quality can be architecturally
significant.

00:02:45.003 --> 00:02:50.131
Prescriptive, or intended, architecture
describes how the system is supposed to be

00:02:50.131 --> 00:02:55.885
organized. Descriptive, or implemented,
architecture describes what actually exists. The
two

00:02:55.885 --> 00:03:01.076
can differ because software evolves,
documentation becomes stale, or implementation

00:03:01.076 --> 00:03:03.264
decisions bypass agreed boundaries.

00:03:03.503 --> 00:03:08.518
Treat an intended diagram as a statement of
design intent, not proof of the current code

00:03:08.518 --> 00:03:09.088
structure.

00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:13.762
Architecture drift means new implementation
decisions are absent from the intended

00:03:13.762 --> 00:03:19.006
description without violating it. Architecture
erosion means the implementation violates the

00:03:19.006 --> 00:03:24.022
intended architecture. Suppose the intended
design permits a presentation module to call

00:03:24.022 --> 00:03:29.095
only an application interface. An allowed new
implementation behind that interface may be

00:03:29.095 --> 00:03:34.339
undocumented drift; a direct presentation-to-
storage dependency violates the stated boundary

00:03:34.339 --> 00:03:35.194
and is erosion.

00:03:35.194 --> 00:03:40.316
Architecture recovery reconstructs an account of
the implemented system from evidence such

00:03:40.316 --> 00:03:45.096
as source code, executable structure,
configuration, and developer knowledge. Record

00:03:45.096 --> 00:03:50.161
uncertainty rather than filling gaps with
guesses. Compare the recovered account with the

00:03:50.161 --> 00:03:55.225
intended one, investigate differences, and
decide whether to repair the implementation or

00:03:55.225 --> 00:03:58.526
deliberately revise the intended design and its
rationale.

00:03:58.694 --> 00:04:03.649
An undocumented component is added within all
permitted dependency rules. Is that

00:04:03.649 --> 00:04:08.970
necessarily erosion? No. Under the distinction
in the slides, a compatible undocumented

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:13.436
decision is drift. Erosion requires evidence of
a violated intended rule.
